      "side": 0,
      "message": "So for the PS8818, I looked up its data sheet yesterday and it boots into a default state of None, so I\u0027d say for that one it kind of counts if we set None while it\u0027s off, in that it will be None as soon as it\u0027s on again.  In our case, we\u0027re going to boot into USB mode instead.  I\u0027m not as sure about the default states for the other chips though.\n\nDenis, if we don\u0027t do this, then the code thinks it correctly set the mux state to None and never bothers re-setting the mux later after we\u0027ve powered on.  At the moment, that creates an increase in power draw which our EE noticed.  \n\nIt will generate error messages on the console definitely if we do this, but if you know more bad things which will happen I can try to see if there\u0027s a better solution.",
